  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 300 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nBy Wednesday, an upper level trough begins to track across the \nwestern United States, which will help usher in a pattern shift to \ncooler and wetter weather. With this trough pushing slowly across \nthe western United States, there will be better support for \nmonsoonal moisture to push north and potentially into portions of \nwestern Nebraska. This will support generally better precipitation \nchances mid week into the weekend across most of the region. \nEnsemble guidance continues to show a fairly strong signal for \nprecipitation across the region, with the European ensembles \nsuggesting higher end precipitation amounts compared to the GEFS. \nAlready some signals begin to suggest additional severe weather \npotential for Wednesday. Instability is still expected to build \nthroughout the day, as temperatures remain near seasonal Wednesday \nafternoon. Later throughout the day, guidance suggests an upper \nlevel shortwave enhancing deep layer shear, which may support \norganized convection should storms initiate. However, some of the \nmore fine scale features will still need to be resolved, especially \ngiven the timing of the trough, to gain higher confidence in the \nexact severe threats and timing. For what it's worth, the Storm \nPrediction Center has already highlighted a portion of western \nNebraska and South Dakota with a 15 percent chance of severe weather \nin the latest outlook for Wednesday. \n\nBeyond Wednesday, near daily precipitation chances are expected with \ntemperatures dropping to below normal. In fact, Thursday into the \nweekend, highs are currently forecast to only reach into the 70s, \nwhich would be around 10 to 15 degrees cooler than average. These \ntemperatures would be at or below the 10th percentile compared to \nlocal climatology, which would be a big flip from some areas pushing \nat or above the 90th percentile earlier in the week. With \ntemperatures remaining on the cooler side and generally cloudy skies \neach day, do think that this will somewhat limit the severe weather \npotential. However, as mentioned earlier, the better moisture \nstreaming into the area may be quite supportive of decent rainfall. \nEnsemble probabilities are right around 40 to 60 percent on most of \nthe area receiving one inch or better (total through the end of the \nweek), with individual runs in the ensembles generally supporting \nthis. With this somewhat higher confidence in heavy rain potential, \nworth mentioning that the Weather Prediction Center has us outlooked \nfor at least a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall Wednesday and \nThursday, with portions of southwest Nebraska in a Slight Risk for \nExcessive Rainfall for Thursday. Will certainly be keeping an eye on \nthese forecast trends as we approach mid to late week. Even though \nthere is some uncertainty in exact amounts, confidence continues to \nbuild in at least daily precipitation chances.",
